    "candidate_text": "For each \\(x\\in[0,1)\\), we have \\(x^n\\to0\\), whereas \\(f_n(1)=1\\) for every \\(n\\). Hence the pointwise limit is\n\\[\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&0\\le x<1,\\\\1,&x=1.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nAssume that \\((f_n)\\) converges uniformly. Uniform convergence implies pointwise convergence, so its uniform limit must be \\(f\\). Set \\(\\varepsilon=\\tfrac14\\). For every \\(N\\in\\mathbb Z_+\\), choose \\(n=N\\) and \\(x=2^{-1/n}\\in[0,1)\\). Then \\(f(x)=0\\), while\n\\[\n|f_n(x)-f(x)|=x^n=\\frac12>\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThus no \\(N\\) satisfies the uniform-convergence condition. Therefore \\((f_n)\\) does not converge uniformly on \\([0,1]\\).",
